Native camera automation authorization
======================================

Before opening a camera adapter, native camera automation resolves the rule's
stored author in the action's fresh database session. The author must exist,
remain active and undeleted, and belong to the rule's organization. Identity in
the action payload cannot replace the trusted execution context.

The action then requires the author's current module permission and the same
site and per-camera/group access checks used by camera access:

  camera.ptz                cameras.ptz       PTZ access
  camera.snapshot           cameras.view      Live access
  camera.motion_detection   cameras.manage    Configure access
  camera.reboot             cameras.manage    Configure access

Explicit site permission ceilings apply too: a read-only site grant cannot
authorize PTZ, motion configuration or reboot; an unknown access level refuses
all four actions. A permitted live snapshot remains a read operation.

Missing authors, permission loss, restricted sites, refused camera grants and
authorization lookup failures return a failed action before adapter creation.
Database/client exception details are not copied into that refusal's execution
history. Authorized operations keep their existing adapter calls and disconnect
handling. Administrators should review old rules with no stored author: those
rules are refused rather than given an implicit system camera identity.

These are authorization checks at action dispatch, not an in-flight cancellation
or exactly-once mechanism. An operation already sent to hardware cannot be
recalled by a later permission change. Rule-cache synchronization, transaction
rollback, staged-write parity and hardware/firmware qualification remain separate
release concerns. This change does not qualify the external camera command's
behavior or claim that every automation action now has equivalent controls.
